# Unanchored on purpose. `/target` matches the root workspace only, and this
# repo has nested workspaces (spikes/*) whose build output landed in git for
# 528 MB across 2057 files before anyone noticed.
target/

# Linux crash core dumps. Deliberately narrower than a bare `core`/`core.*`,
# which would silently swallow a future core.rs module or core/ directory.
# BOTH shapes are needed: the multi-field default (core.%e.%p.%h.%t) needs two
# dots, and the very common `core_pattern = core.%p` produces `core.5821` with
# only one — which the two-dot pattern alone left untracked-but-not-ignored, so
# `git add -A` would happily stage a crash dump.
core.[0-9]*
core.*.[0-9]*

# doc-check tooling (scripts/) — JS deps, not part of the Rust build
scripts/node_modules/

# macOS Finder droppings. The history directories get browsed in Finder, so they
# collect these. No check rejects one — the shared walker returns only .md and
# the register scan skips dotfiles — which is the point: nothing but this line
# stands between a `git add -A` and a committed .DS_Store.
.DS_Store

# Unanchored: spikes/ are standalone workspaces, and one opened as its own IDE
# project root puts .idea/ there rather than at the repo root.
#
# BOTH editors, because the doc walker skips both: a tracked `.vscode/notes.md`
# would be committed by `git add -A` and then scanned by nothing — no link check,
# no size cap — while the identical file under .idea/ could not be committed at
# all. Skipped and ignored have to be the same set.
.idea/
.vscode/
